  "query_preview": "..(Monday night through Saturday) \nIssued at 750 AM EDT SUN AUG 16 2026\n\nFollowing the frontal passage Monday evening, drier air will \nadvect in from the north for Monday night. By Tuesday, dewpoints \nwill be only in the mid to upper 60s, with perhaps a few areas in \nthe 70s in the vicinity of Lake Cumberland. Highs will also be \nmarginally cooler, generally in the mid to upper 80s, and rain \nchances are looking to be low. Of note, a few recent model runs do\ndepict subtle shortwave energy traversing the area, which could \ninspire a few showers or storms, although this scenario still \nappears unlikely at this time.\n\nThe next robust rain chances will arrive Wednesday evening into \nthe overnight hours. Aloft, troughing will continue to dig into \nthe Great Lakes region, and the ridge to the south will continue \nto flatten. Meanwhile, an additional cold front will push \nsoutheast, with rain chances ticking up again late Wednesday. \nMoisture will return quite quickly as winds become southwesterly \nahead of the front, and PW values look to leap again too (with \nLREF mean PW in the 1.6-1.7 in range for much of the area, \nrepresenting at least the 75th percentile compared to \nclimatology). As in previous days, this environment could support \nat least an isolated flash flooding risk; WPC has again placed the\nmajority of the forecast area in a Day 4/5 Marginal (Level 1/4) \nRisk for Excessive Rainfall.\n\nBehind the front, temperatures will be noticeably cooler. With\neastern Kentucky sitting near the base of the upper level trough,\nflow aloft will either be zonal or northwesterly, with models \nstill disagreeing about the exact orientation of the trough at \nthis stage in the forecast. Regardless, this pattern will open \neastern Kentucky up to additional shortwave impulses. Although \nexact timing is difficult to resolve at this timescale, broad rain\nchances have been maintained each day through the end of the \nperiod for now.",
